# infra/observability/langfuse/images.env -- the image tags of the four datastores Langfuse v3
# requires beside it. Named from infra/versions.env by LANGFUSE_STACK_IMAGES_FILE, exactly as
# BOUTIQUE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE names infra/boutique/images.yaml, so there is one path from the
# single pinning surface to every pin and this file is not a second surface.
#
# WHY THESE FOUR ARE NOT KEYS IN infra/versions.env
#   That file's groups follow the layer table in docs/03-tech-stack.md section 2, and Postgres,
#   ClickHouse, Redis and MinIO are none of those layers. They are Langfuse's internal dependencies:
#   this project neither queries them nor reads a number out of them, and swapping the trace plane
#   would retire all four at once. Pinning them here keeps them beside the compose file that is
#   their only reader, while LANGFUSE_STACK_IMAGES_FILE keeps them findable from the one place a
#   reader looks for a version.
#
# WHY THEY ARE PINNED AT ALL, GIVEN UPSTREAM DOES NOT
#   The upstream docker-compose.yml at v3.225.5 pins none of the four to a patch: ClickHouse runs on
#   a bare repository name, Redis on `7`, Postgres on `17` and MinIO on a Chainguard image with no
#   public version tag. Every one of those is a moving target, so an upstream compose brought up six
#   months apart is two different stacks. NFR1 is the reproducibility claim the whole pinning
#   discipline exists to support, and it does not stop at the boundary of this project's own code:
#   a trace plane that silently changed its storage engine between the pilot and the campaign is a
#   difference nothing in a run bundle would record.
#
# FORMAT
#   Values only, one key per line, no logic and no secrets -- infra/versions.env's rule 1, applied
#   here because this file is read the same way: `docker compose --env-file`. Credentials live in
#   .env, which is gitignored; see this directory's README.md.
#
# HOW EACH TAG WAS CHOSEN
#   The floor is upstream's own compatibility statement for Langfuse v3, and the tag is the current
#   patch on the line upstream itself runs, so this is a pin rather than a version bump. Postgres
#   and Redis stay on the major upstream's compose names -- 17 and 7 -- because moving a major to
#   chase a newer patch would be a behaviour change dressed as a pin; Redis 8 in particular trips
#   Langfuse's BullMQ version check, which upstream papers over with a skip flag.

LANGFUSE_POSTGRES_TAG=17.11
LANGFUSE_CLICKHOUSE_TAG=25.8.33.6
LANGFUSE_REDIS_TAG=7.4.11
LANGFUSE_MINIO_TAG=RELEASE.2025-09-07T16-13-09Z
